┗┻┛┗┻┛┗━╸╹ ╹┗━╸  ╹┗━┛   ┗━┛┗━╸┗┛

multiple-choice questions for https://huggingface.co/unsloth/Qwen3.8-27B-GGUF


a json grammar makes the model write a valid answer.
this skips the writing and reads the odds of each answer instead.

    llm.decide(state, "did the deployment succeed?", ["yes", "no", "unclear"])
    -> {"yes": 0.91, "no": 0.03, "unclear": 0.06}


quick demo:

    $ uv run example.py


benchmark, 38 graded items over one incident report, jev (read the odds) vs
plain (let the model write the letter), with and without thinking first,
on a 3090 Ti:

    $ uv run bench.py

    mode          accuracy  p(expected)   s/item   total s
    ------------------------------------------------------
    jev             94.7%        0.932     0.59      22.3
    jev think      100.0%        1.000     2.77     105.1
    plain           97.4%            -     0.34      12.9
    plain think    100.0%            -     2.74     104.0
    ------------------------------------------------------
    jev and plain pick the same answer on 92.1% of items, think=False
    jev and plain pick the same answer on 100.0% of items, think=True


References:

- OpenJev, the same idea on a 4B model: https://github.com/TheoLeeCJ/openjev
- Jev, the closed service it reproduces: https://typesafe.ai
- Qwen3.8-27B: https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen3.8-27B
